                        "review": "MXGP3 is an enjoyable MX experience, but it lacks the challenge and depth needed to hold my attention beyond a single MX2 career playthrough. That's not to say its simpler arcade riding experience is inherently bad, in many ways I actually enjoyed the change of pace from the slippery handling Milestone introduced in its later entries. I just found myself getting bored after a handful of career races. I'd imagine experiencing the game during its peak with an active multiplayer community would have made it considerably more engaging.\n\nI'm also not sure how fair this criticism is nearly a decade later but for an Unreal Engine 4 title the game looks strangely blurry, dark, and visually basic. Disabling bloom and motion blur helps, but the harsh contrast remains. I'd recommend installing ReShade to give the game a bit of a visual facelift.\n\nOverall, I'd give MXGP3 a 6.5/10. It's a decent game, but playing it in 2026 I'm not entirely convinced it's the magnum opus some people make it out to be. Fortunately you can buy MXGP3 for almost nothing on sale, so if you want some cheap MX fun from Milestone this is a decent starting point.",
